Let json-c 0.15 configure under cmake 4.

TOOLCHAIN COMPATIBILITY FLAG, NOT A CUT. Nothing is removed from the build, no
declared dependency changes, and the package that comes out is what json-c intends.
It is deliberately not filed with the stage-1 cuts.

## The failure

    CMake Error at CMakeLists.txt:3 (cmake_minimum_required):
      Compatibility with CMake < 3.5 has been removed from CMake.

      Update the VERSION argument <min> value.  Or, use the <min>...<max> syntax
      to tell CMake that the project requires at least <min> but has been updated
      to work with policies introduced by <max> or earlier.

      Or, add -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 to try configuring anyway.

    -- Configuring incomplete, errors occurred!

json-c 0.15 predates that removal and declares a minimum below 3.5. cmake 4.1.6 now
refuses it outright, at `CMakeLists.txt:3` — before looking at compilers, headers or
anything else. So this is not a portability problem with the code; the configure step
never gets far enough to have one.

## The fix

    -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5

which restores the pre-3.5 policy defaults, i.e. exactly the behaviour cmake 3.x gave
this project when it was released and tested. The alternative — editing
`cmake_minimum_required` in the upstream `CMakeLists.txt` — changes the same thing
less honestly, since it would claim the project had been reviewed against newer
policies when it has not.

Preferred over bumping the port to a newer json-c because the tree's only other
recipe is `json_c4-0.13.1`, which is *older* and has the same problem.

## Why this port is needed here

`hubbub`, NetSurf's HTML parser, has `devel:libjson_c` in `BUILD_REQUIRES`, and
`hubbub` is on the critical path to `netsurf-3.11`. Without json-c the browser chain
stops one wave short.

## Verification

`json_c-0.15-4-arm64.hpkg` (28,028 B), `json_c_devel` (27,333 B) and
`json_c_debuginfo` (57,865 B), `_dirty` requirement count **0** on all three, and the
devel package provides what hubbub actually asks for:

    provides: devel:libjson_c = 5.1.0 (compatible >= 5)
    requires: json_c==0.15

Watch for this in other pre-2020 cmake ports; it is a property of cmake 4, not of
json-c, so any recipe carrying an old `cmake_minimum_required` will hit it.

Applies to: haikuports/dev-libs/json_c/json_c-0.15.recipe (tree copy -- json_c has no
input source package, so the tree recipe is the operative one)

--- a/json_c-0.15.recipe
+++ b/json_c-0.15.recipe
@@ BUILD()
 BUILD()
 {
+	# graviton: json-c 0.15 declares cmake_minimum_required(VERSION < 3.5), and cmake 4
+	# removed compatibility with < 3.5 outright, so configure dies at CMakeLists.txt:3
+	# before looking at anything else. This flag restores the pre-3.5 policy defaults,
+	# i.e. exactly what cmake 3.x did with this project. It removes nothing from the
+	# build and changes no declared dependency -- the package that comes out is what
+	# json-c intends -- so it is a toolchain compatibility flag, not a content cut.
+	# Drop it when the recipe is updated to a json-c release that declares a cmake
+	# 3.5+ minimum. Needed because hubbub build-requires devel:libjson_c.
 	cmake -Bbuild -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
+		-DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 \
 		$cmakeDirArgs . \
 		-DENABLE_THREADING=ON \
 		-DENABLE_RDRAND=OFF \
 		-DBUILD_STATIC_LIBS=OFF

 	cmake --build build $jobArgs
 }
